secretary of state there is a new  assembly in northern ireland but little  prospect it seems of a new government so  how do you envisage this place being  governed in the throes of a cost of  living crisis  well first of all david i think all of  us want to see storm up and running i  think it goes without saying as i said  yesterday i think the people of northern  who voted want their politicians to take  part in the democracy that they voted in  so i'll be encouraging the party leaders  when we meet with them tomorrow as i  have done over the last few weeks and  indeed in the contact i've had with them  over the last few days to to get those  nominations in let's get the assembly  speaker elected and get the assembly up  and running there's a lot of decisions  to make for the benefit of people in  northern ireland there's money uk  governments put into the northern  executive we want to spend in northern  ireland and that means we need to get  those positions filled as quickly as  possible  but the democratic unionists have made  it abundantly clear that they've no  intention of restoring power sharing  until the government addresses their  concerns over the northern ireland  protocol that a controversial border in  the irish sea are you going to do that  in the queen's speech on tuesday  well i recognise the challenges with the  northern protocol for people across  unionism and actually the protocol is  causing problems for people across  northern ireland it's not just about  unionist parties or any one party  certainly affects communities across  northern ireland and businesses as well  regardless of their voting intentions  and we need to get that resolved it is  really frustrating that the eu have not  shown the flexibility we need to see to  get that resolution it's more  frustrating i have to say to hear over  the last couple of days that the eu seem  to be saying they're not willing to show  any any any sort of flexibility that we  need to get this resolved  we do think the right resolution is an  agreement with the eu we've always been  focused on that that's why we've not  triggered article 16 yet but we've got  to be clear you know the protocol that  was designed to protect the good friday  agreement is what is actually putting  the most stress on it at the moment we  can't let that continue we do need to  get a resolution to this we've always  said we take nothing off the table and  that hasn't changed we will do what we  need to do to resolve these issues for  the benefit of people in northern  ireland  you say you take nothing off the table  that obviously includes the possibility  of unilateral action but the prime  minister told the dup there would never  be a border in the irish sea when he  conceded on that you tried to pretend  there wasn't a border in the rsc now  you're saying the government's going to  address concerns over the border in the  irish sea so why would the democratic  unionists believe this government  well if you look back at the beginning  of this uh when i made the point about  we don't want to see them we didn't  think there'd be a border in the irc  that one of the issues was actually  about the implementation the protocol  the eu's uh way and style of wanting to  implement some of these things that we  had grace periods put in place  specifically to allow these issues to be  worked out they've never shown the  flexibility to properly resolve in the  way that we always i have today thought  that they would and the reality is this  is causing problems our job as a  government is to recognize that and to  want to deal with it it's causing  problems at a number of levels there are  communities who can't access goods and  products in the way they need to there's  businesses who are affected in the same  way that affects the whole of the uk  businesses in all the night who can't  get supplies businesses in great britain  who are not supplying northern that  affects everybody and obviously having  that pressure on east west that pressure  on stormon and the fact that stormland  isn't sitting is putting that pressure  and damage on the good friday agreement  itself we've always been very clear our  priority is and will always be the  protection of the good friday agreement  and that's why it's important we do  resolve these issues and we need to do  that  with all of the focus on the concerns of  the democratic unionist party it  sometimes creates the impression that  most people in northern ireland have  concerns about the brexit border in the  irish sea but that's not the reality of  course of the 90 people  elected to the new northern ireland  assembly 54 of them are in favor of the  protocol being implemented perhaps with  some adjustments so why is the  government really only interested in  dancing to the dups tune  well i think that's a bit of a  a misnomer that's out there i've got to  say and you've just said david actually  all of the parties now recognize and are  open with people about the fact there  are problems with the protocol i accept  they have different views about how  extreme those issues are but all the  parties want to see issues with the  protocol resolved they accept there are  problems there  pretty much every sector of the business  community almost all but one have been  very clear with us and with the eu and  others that the protocol doesn't work  the eu's offer of september last year  doesn't work they've got frustration i  think with the eu not properly engaging  with the kind of flexibilities that  would resolve these issues as we do so  it is clear that this is an issue that  affects everybody in northern ireland  and our job as uk government is to make  sure that we rectify that we want to do  that with the eu that's why our focus  has been on that rather than triggering  article 16 which we could have done last  year but there is a point at which we  will need to make some decisions around  if the eu is refusing to show  flexibility what can we do to alleviate  these issues for the benefit of citizens  of the united kingdom the people of all  communities in northern ireland  finally and very briefly there's a  nationalist first minister in scotland  there now is a nationalist first  minister elect in northern ireland is  this historic vote not proof positive  that brexit has threatened the future of  the union and could lead to the breakup  of the united kingdom  i think if we look at the facts of  what's happened over the last couple  days no doubt this is a significant  election particularly for northern  ireland because we do have a nationalist  uh elect first minister  but we also have to remember obviously  the shin of fame vote hasn't  changed their number of seats they've  went in with 27 seats they've still got  27 seats the overall vote  is very clearly there for uh for  unionism as a block it still is the  largest group uh stormont and and and  the voting on that pattern hasn't really  changed what has also been significant i  think as the person who's on the show  just before me is outlined there's been  that increase in vote and the increase  in seats that we've seen for the  alliance so i think this has been a  significant election but we also need to  be very clear i think the people in  northern their main focus what they'll  expect all of us as elected politicians  to do is get on with dealing with those  issues that affect them every day they  want to see the health service reforms  and investments in northern ireland  we've put the support in there for as uk  government they want to see the economy  growing as we recover from covid and yes  they want us to resolve those protocol  issues that i think is where the focus  for all of us needs to be in the period  ahead
 

